    "rendered": "<div id='gallery-1' class='gallery'><figure class='gallery-item'>\n\t\t\t<div class='gallery-icon landscape'>\n\t\t\t\t<a data-elementor-open-lightbox=\"yes\" data-elementor-lightbox-slideshow=\"7b67f80\" data-elementor-lightbox-title=\"Yankees Tigers Gehrig Ends Streak\" e-action-hash=\"#elementor-action%3Aaction%3Dlightbox%26settings%3DeyJpZCI6MTQ3LCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczpcL1wvbG91Z2VocmlnLmNvbVwvd3AtY29udGVudFwvdXBsb2Fkc1wvMjAyMVwvMDlcL2dlaGwwMjguanBnIiwic2xpZGVzaG93IjoiN2I2N2Y4MCJ9\" href='https://lougehrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/gehl028.jpg' title=\"\" data-rl_title=\"\" class=\"rl-gallery-link\" data-rl_caption=\"\" data-rel=\"lightbox-gallery-1\"><img width=\"150\" height=\"150\" src=\"https://lougehrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/gehl028-150x150.jpg\" class=\"attachment-thumbnail size-thumbnail\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" aria-describedby=\"gallery-1-147\" /></a>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t<figcaption class='wp-caption-text gallery-caption' id='gallery-1-147'>\n\t\t\t\tYankee first baseman Lou Gehrig watches from the dugout May 2, 1939 as his teammates warm up for their game with the Tigers in Detroit. The Iron Horse told manager Joe McCarthy he was benching himself for the good of the team, bringing his 2,130 game streak to an end. The Briggs Stadium fans gave Gehrig a standing ovation when they learned he would not be in the lineup. The Yankees stormed to a 22-2 win. (AP Photo)\n\t\t\t\t</figcaption></figure><figure class='gallery-item'>\n\t\t\t<div class='gallery-icon landscape'>\n\t\t\t\t<a data-elementor-open-lightbox=\"yes\" data-elementor-lightbox-slideshow=\"7b67f80\" data-elementor-lightbox-title=\"Lou Gehrig Following Through 1938\" e-action-hash=\"#elementor-action%3Aaction%3Dlightbox%26settings%3DeyJpZCI6MTYzLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczpcL1wvbG91Z2VocmlnLmNvbVwvd3AtY29udGVudFwvdXBsb2Fkc1wvMjAyMVwvMDlcL1AwMDktUEQtR2VocmlnLV8yMS5qcGciLCJzbGlkZXNob3ciOiI3YjY3ZjgwIn0%3D\" href='https://lougehrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/P009-PD-Gehrig-_21.jpg' title=\"\" data-rl_title=\"\" class=\"rl-gallery-link\" data-rl_caption=\"\" data-rel=\"lightbox-gallery-1\"><img width=\"150\" height=\"150\" src=\"https://lougehrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/P009-PD-Gehrig-_21-150x150.jpg\" class=\"attachment-thumbnail size-thumbnail\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" aria-describedby=\"gallery-1-163\" /></a>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t<figcaption class='wp-caption-text gallery-caption' id='gallery-1-163'>\n\t\t\t\tCHICAGO &#8211; 1938.  Lou Gehrig whacks a double into left center in a game at Yankee Stadium in 1938.  Luke Sewell is the catcher for the opponent White Sox.  (Photo by Mark Rucker/Transcendental Graphics, Getty Images)\n\t\t\t\t</figcaption></figure><figure class='gallery-item'>\n\t\t\t<div class='gallery-icon portrait'>\n\t\t\t\t<a data-elementor-open-lightbox=\"yes\" data-elementor-lightbox-slideshow=\"7b67f80\" data-elementor-lightbox-title=\"gehl008\" e-action-hash=\"#elementor-action%3Aaction%3Dlightbox%26settings%3DeyJpZCI6MTQxLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczpcL1wvbG91Z2VocmlnLmNvbVwvd3AtY29udGVudFwvdXBsb2Fkc1wvMjAyMVwvMDlcL2dlaGwwMDguanBnIiwic2xpZGVzaG93IjoiN2I2N2Y4MCJ9\" href='https://lougehrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/gehl008.jpg' title=\"\" data-rl_title=\"\" class=\"rl-gallery-link\" data-rl_caption=\"\" data-rel=\"lightbox-gallery-1\"><img width=\"150\" height=\"150\" src=\"https://lougehrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/gehl008-150x150.jpg\" class=\"attachment-thumbnail size-thumbnail\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></a>\n\t\t\t</div></figure></div>"
